window.docContents[67].resolve({"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.PRNG.Defs/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___PRNG___Defs":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/PRNG › Defs.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof 1 headline theorem depend on it 121 of 200 lines trusted trust banner B2, trusted: definitions used by the statement source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nDeclarations on this page (15)structure PRFPRNGabbrev specabbrev AdversaryCompabbrev Adversarynotation \"Comp\"structure GameStatenotation \"GameM\"def samIfNecdef processImpldef challPrngImpldef challPrfImpldef corrImpldef oracleImpldef gamedef advantage\n\nPRF-PRNGs: syntax and security gameTrusted: definitions used by the statement. The theorems of Computational/Theorems/ use every definition in this file: read them. The theorems here are checked by Lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.Defines PRF-PRNGs (PRFPRNG, ACD19 §4.3.1 Def. 16) and their combined PRNG/PRF hidden-bit security game (PRFPRNG.game, PRFPRNG.advantage, Def. 17 and Fig. 7). In PRNG mode (processImpl, challPrngImpl), outputs are pseudorandom whenever the state is uncorrupted or healed by a uniform input (none); in PRF mode (challPrfImpl), up σ · on an uncorrupted state without state update is indistinguishable from a lazily sampled random function.\n\n\n\nstructure PRFPRNGL37–43 ↗#ACD19 Def. 16: a PRF-PRNG with initialization keys Key, states State, inputs Input, and outputs Output.\n\n\n\nabbrev specL47–53 ↗#ACD19 Fig. 7 oracle interface (none = refused; Option Input = none denotes uniform input sampling ⊥).\n\nabbrev AdversaryCompL55–57 ↗#Computations with own coins plus the Fig. 7 PRF-PRNG oracles.\n\nabbrev AdversaryL59–60 ↗#A PRF-PRNG adversary outputting a hidden-bit guess.\n\n\n\nnotation \"Comp\"L66 ↗#\n\nstructure GameStateL70–89 ↗#ACD19 Fig. 7 game state.\n\n\n\nnotation \"GameM\"L95 ↗#\n\ndef samIfNecL97–104 ↗#Fig. 7 sam-if-nec(I): return (I, false) if I is provided, or sample a uniform I and return (I, true).\n\ndef processImplL106–120 ↗#Fig. 7 process(I): absorb I (healing corr if I = none) and return R; refused once prf is set.DEV-PRNG2 (paper fix): PRF mode freezes the state. In ACD19 Fig. 7, process stays available after chall-prf. Then the game is trivially insecure: chall-prf(I) returns the real next state σ', process(I) moves the game to σ', and chall-prf(I₂) on σ' is answered with F(I₂) or P-Up(σ', I₂).2, which the adversary can compute. Here process is refused once prf is set. Effect: none for the SM proof: ACD19 Lemma 10 continues from the returned σ' by itself.\n\ndef challPrngImplL122–134 ↗#Fig. 7 chall-prng(I): require an uncorrupted state (after samIfNec) and ¬prf; update σ and return R (b = false) or a uniform output (b = true).\n\ndef challPrfImplL136–152 ↗#Fig. 7 chall-prf(I): require ¬corr and ¬prng; evaluate (σ', R) = P.up σ I without updating σ, returning (σ', R) (b = false) or (σ', F(I)) (b = true).\n\ndef corrImplL154–164 ↗#Fig. 7 corr: reveal σ and mark corr := true; refused once prf is set.DEV-PRNG1 (paper fix): corr sets the flag corr. ACD19 Fig. 7's corr never sets corr ← true (only init and sam-if-nec assign it), so req ¬corr in the challenge oracles would be vacuous and the game trivially insecure (corrupt, then compute P-Up yourself). Here corr sets it, as the text intends (\"the attacker may obtain the state\"). Effect: none (it repairs the game).\n\ndef oracleImplL166–172 ↗#Combined Fig. 7 oracle implementation.DEV-PRNG3 (formalization): req, as in ACD19 §2.1. A failing req returns none and leaves the state unchanged, including the sampling of I by sam-if-nec (whose effect corr ← false is then not applied). This is ACD19 §2.1's convention (\"all actions by it are undone\"). Effect: none.\n\n\n\ndef gameL183–190 ↗#ACD19 Fig. 7 hidden-bit game.\n\ndef advantageL192–201 ↗#Adv^{PP}_P(A) (ACD19 Def. 17): the bias 2·|Pr[b = b'] − 1/2| (DEV-SM10).\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/PRNG/Defs.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.PRNG.Defs/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___PRNG___Defs"}});